 ## Shared principles
 All methods evaluate a mapping function at sample points $\mathbf{x}$ within the volume to produce visual attributes [G]. The core mechanism involves:
 1. **Input Evaluation**: Sampling the data field $f(\mathbf{x})$ and potentially derived features (e.g., gradients, eigenvalues) at position $\mathbf{x}$ [G].
-2. **Mapping**: Applying a function to convert these inputs into visual parameters. For scalar data, this yields opacity $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ and color $\mathbf{c} \in [0, 1]^3$ [38-classification-and-transfer-functions.md](38-classification-and-transfer-functions.md). For vector/tensor data, it yields directional cues or geometric primitives [39-multi-field-vector-and-tensor-volume-visualization.md](39-multi-field-vector-and-tensor-volume-visualization.md).
